{"name":"Bora Bora Shore","hex":"#92d0d0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bora","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An initiation ceremony for males among the Aboriginal people of New South Wales."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cold, often dry, northeasterly wind which blows, sometimes in violent gusts, down from mountains on the eastern shore of the Adriatic Sea. It also applies to cold, squally, downslope winds in other parts of the world."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bora"},{"term":"shore","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Land adjoining a non-flowing body of water, such as an ocean, lake or pond.","Land, usually near a port."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To arrive at the shore","To put ashore."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shore"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bora Bora","blocks":[{"text":"Bora Bora is a French island group in the Leeward Islands, comprising the western part of the Society Islands of French Polynesia, an overseas collectivity of the French Republic in the South Pacific. Bora Bora has a total land area of 30.55 km2 (12 sq mi). The main island, located about 230 kilometres northwest of Papeete, is surrounded by a lagoon and a barrier reef. In the center of the island are the remnants of an extinct volcano, rising to two peaks, Mount Pahia and Mount Otemanu; the highest point is at 727 m (2,385 ft)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Bora_Bora_ISS006.jpg/330px-Bora_Bora_ISS006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bora_Bora","wikidata":"Q183113"},{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast","wikidata":"Q93352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Societe_isl_BoraBora.PNG/330px-Societe_isl_BoraBora.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Societe_isl_BoraBora.PNG/330px-Societe_isl_BoraBora.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASociete_isl_BoraBora.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Terii_Maeva_Rua%2C_Queen_of_Bora_Bora.jpg/330px-Terii_Maeva_Rua%2C_Queen_of_Bora_Bora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Terii_Maeva_Rua%2C_Queen_of_Bora_Bora.jpg/330px-Terii_Maeva_Rua%2C_Queen_of_Bora_Bora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Teriimaevarua III, last Queen of Bora Bora","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATerii_Maeva_Rua%2C_Queen_of_Bora_Bora.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Bora_Bora_North-East_view_from_Mt_Pahia_-_French_Polynesia.jpg/500px-Bora_Bora_North-East_view_from_Mt_Pahia_-_French_Polynesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Bora_Bora_North-East_view_from_Mt_Pahia_-_French_Polynesia.jpg/1280px-Bora_Bora_North-East_view_from_Mt_Pahia_-_French_Polynesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&ut